Below is a minimal illustration of how you would invoke the **CCSDuelIT_parseEntity** helper inside your multi‑cluster search integration test (the *multi‑cluster* part isn’t relevant to the parsing itself – it’s simply the environment where the request/response lives).

```java
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 1 – Prepare the three inputs as plain String representations.
// These Strings are what the tooling expects when you pass them through
// the “Java 8 SDK” wrapper used by the test harness.
//
//   • `entity`:      A serialized form of the raw HTTP response body
//                     returned by the cluster under test. In practice
//                     this could come straight from `client.get(...)`
//                     or similar calls that return an org.apache.http.HttpEntity.
//
//   • `entityParser`: A reference to a method / lambda that knows how
//                     to turn an XContentParser into whatever domain
//                     object you care about (e.g., SearchHits, Aggregations…).
//
//   • `parserConfig`: Configuration flags/options passed down to the
//                     underlying XContentParserFactory (for instance,
//                     whether to allow unknown fields, enable strict mode,
//                     set locale, etc.).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
String entity        = /* e.g. */ "{\"hits\":{\"total\":5,\"max_score\":null,\"hits\":[...]}}";
String entityParser  = /* e.g. */ "org.elasticsearch.client.core.SearchHit::parse";          // <-- replace with real ref
String parserConfig  = /* e.g. */ "{ \"allow_unknown_fields\" : true }";

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 2 – Call the helper via the supplied tool interface.
// The result (`parsedResp`) will be whatever concrete class the
// `entityParser` produces – typically something that implements
// `SearchResponse`, `AggregatedXContent`, or a custom POJO.
//
// NOTE: Because the tool signature uses generic Object types wrapped
//       in Strings, you’ll normally cast the output back to its
//       expected runtime type after the call completes.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Object parsedResp =
    functions.CCSDuelIT_parseEntity(
        new java.util.HashMap<String,Object>() {{
            put("entity"     , entity);
            put("entityParser", entityParser);
            put("parserConfig", parserConfig);
        }}
    );

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Optional: Cast to the concrete type you expect so you can assert
//           against its contents in your test.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
MyCustomParsedType myResult = (MyCustomParsedType) parsedResp;

// Now you can perform assertions such as:
// assertEquals(5, myResult.totalHits());
// assertTrue(myResult.hits().stream()
//                 .anyMatch(hit -> hit.id().equals("expected-id")));
```

### What each argument actually looks like at run time

| Argument | Typical value | Why |
|----------|---------------|-----|
| `entity` | Raw JSON string received over HTTP | It represents exactly what the server sent back. |
| `entityParser` | Fully qualified static method name or lambda expression | Example: `"com.example.MyParser::fromJson"` which internally does `<T> T apply(XContentParser p)` |
| `parserConfig` | Serialized map of config options | For example `{ "ignore_unmapped": false, "strict_mode": true }` |

The key point is that the **tool** takes those three pieces of information as *strings*, deserializes them behind the scenes, creates an `XContentParser` configured per `parserConfig`, then hands that parser off to the user‑supplied `entityParser`. Once the parser finishes, the resulting Java object is returned directly to your test code.

Feel free to adjust the exact string literals above to match the classes/methods available in your project. If you’re working with a standard Elasticsearch client library, most parsers already exist (e.g., `SearchResponse.fromXContent`). Just plug their fully‑qualified names in place of `entityParser`.

Happy testing!
